# Supabase Environment Variables

Sprint 13 wires the frontend to Supabase for authentication and the database
layer. Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in your Supabase project values.

## Required

| Variable                | Description                                   | Example                          |
| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------- |
| `VITE_SUPABASE_URL`     | Your Supabase project URL.                    | `https://xyzcompany.supabase.co` |
| `VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`| The public **anon** key from Project Settings. | `eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIs...`        |

> The anon key is safe to expose in the browser. Row Level Security (RLS)
> policies on the Supabase project enforce per-user data access.

## Where to find them

1. Open your [Supabase dashboard](https://supabase.com/dashboard).
2. Select your project.
3. Go to **Project Settings → API**.
4. Copy the **Project URL** (`VITE_SUPABASE_URL`) and the **anon public** key
   (`VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`).

## Example

```bash
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=https://xyzcompany.supabase.co
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...
```

## Next steps after setup

1. Run the SQL migration in `database/migrations/0001_init.sql` against your
   Supabase project (SQL Editor or `supabase db push`).
2. Restart the dev server so the new env vars are picked up:
   ```bash
   cd frontend
   npm run dev
   ```
3. Sign up a user — a `profiles` row is auto-created by the `handle_new_user`
   trigger.

## Notes

- Never commit `.env.local` (it is git-ignored).
- The existing Spring backend JWT auth remains in place for feature pages
  (resume/ATS). Supabase Auth is the single source of truth for logging in and
  signing up.
